<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-9-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Conic Sections: Hyperbolas</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this Lesson we consider hyperbolas and categorize the conic sections. You might want to start class with a calculator demonstration:  solve <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>  for <m>y</m> and graph in a squre window, then do the same for <m>y^2 - x^2 = 1</m> and <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m>.  If your class is strong enough, you can come back to these equations and graphs when you discuss the asymptotes of a hyperbola.</p>
    
<p>You might also point out to the class that we are not exploring the geometric properties of parabolas in this course, but that by interchanging <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in the standard equation, we obtain parabolas that open left or right.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Write the equation for a hyperbola in standard form </p></li>   
    <li><p>Graph a hyperbola</p></li>
    <li><p>Find the equation for a hyperbola</p></li>    
</ul></p></assemblage>

<activity><title>Central Hyperbolas</title>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>Write the two standard equations for central hyperbolas.</p>
  <p><ol marker="1.">
    <li><p>(opens left and right)</p>
        <p></p></li>
    <li><p>(opens up and down)</p>
        <p></p></li>
  </ol></p>
  <p>The segment joining the two vertices is called the <fillin/>.</p>
  <p>The perpendicular segment is called the <fillin/>.</p></li>

<li><p>Follow the steps to graph the hyperbola  <m>~\dfrac{x^2}{9} - \dfrac{y^2}{16} = 1</m></p>.
      <p><ol marker="1.">
        <li><p>Complete the table and plot the vertices.</p>
            <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>y</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>
        <li><p>Plot the two points <m>(0,b)</m> and <m>(0,-b)</m>. These points are not on the hyperbola -- we will use them, along with the vertices, to sketch the central rectangle.</p>
            <image source="act-9-4-1.png" width="60%"><description>Grid [-8,8]x[-8,8]</description></image></li>  
        <li><p>Sketch the asymptotes through the diagonals of the central rectangle.</p></li>
        <li><p>Finally, sketch the two branches of the hyperbola through the vertices and approaching the asymptotes. (Neither the central rectangle nor the asymptotes are part of the graph; they are just tools.)</p></li>
      </ol></p>
          <aside component="instructor"><p>1(c) After students have graphed ellipses, they do not find hyperbolas any harder. Do point out that the branches of a hyperbola are not parabolas, but instead become closer to linear as they approach their asymptotes.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>Follow the steps to graph  <m>~~-4x^2 + 25y^2 = 100</m></p>
    <p><ol marker="1.">
      <li><p>Write the equation in standard form:  divide both sides by 100.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify the values of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Plot the vertices <m>(0,b)</m> and <m>(0,-b)</m>, and the two points <m>(a,0)</m> and <m>(-a,0)</m>.</p>
            <image source="act-9-4-1.png" width="60%"><description>Grid [-8,8]x[-8,8]</description></image></li>
      <li><p>Sketch the central rectangle.</p></li>
      <li><p>Sketch the asymptotes.</p></li>
      <li><p>Sketch the hyperbola.</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Translated Hyperbolas</title>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>The standard forms for a hyperbolas centered at <m>(h,k)</m> are</p>
<p><ol marker="1.">
  <li><p></p>
      <p></p></li>
  <li><p></p>
      <p></p></li>
  </ol></p></li>

<li><p>Follow the steps to graph  <m>~~\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \dfrac{(y+1)^2}{9} = 1</m>.</p>
<p><ol marker="1.">
  <li><p>Identify the values of the center <m>(h,k)</m> and <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p>
      <p><m>~~~~~~(h,k) = </m></p>
      <p><m>~~~~~~a=</m></p>
      <p><m>~~~~~~b=</m></p></li>                                               
  <li><p>Plot the vertices and the ends of the conjugate axis.</p>
    <image source="act-9-4-1.png" width="60%"><description>Grid [-8,8]x[-8,8]</description></image></li>
  <li><p>Sketch the central rectangle.</p></li>
  <li><p>Sketch the asymptotes.</p></li>
  <li><p>Sketch the hyperbola.</p></li>
</ol></p></li>

<li><p>Find the equations of the asymptotes. The asymptotes pass through the center of the hyperbola, so</p>
        <aside component="instructor"><p>2(c) Finding the equations of the asymptotes is a good review of using point-slope formula.</p></aside>
      <p><m>~~(x_1, y_1) = (h,k) = </m></p>
  <p>Look at the central rectangle to see that the slopes of the asymptotes are</p>
      <p><m>~~m = \dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x} = \dfrac{\pm b}{a} = </m></p>
  <p>Substitute these values into the point-slope form and find the equations of the asymptotes.</p></li>

<li><p>On the same grid above, sketch the graph of <me>~~\dfrac{(y+1)^2}{9} - \dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} = 1</me></p>
        <aside component="instructor"><p>2(d) Try to use different colored pens for the graph; many students find this exercise aesthetically pleasing.</p></aside>  
  <p>How does the graph differ from the first graph?</p>
  <p>Finally, on the same grid sketch the graph of <me>~~\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + \dfrac{(y+1)^2}{9} = 1</me></p>
  <p>Very nice.</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Standard Form</title>

<p>Follow the steps to write the equation  
      <md>4x^2-6y^2+16x-12y-14=0</md>
  in standard form.</p>
<p><ol marker="1."> 
    <li><p>Collect the <m>x</m>-terms and <m>y</m>-terms together.
        <md><fillin characters="16"/> = 14</md></p></li>                                               
    <li><p>Factor out the coefficients of <m>x^2</m> and <m>y^2</m>. Be careful:  you are factoring a 
    negative number from the <m>y^2</m>-terms.                
        <md><fillin characters="16"/> = 14</md></p></li>
    <li><p>Complete the square in <m>x</m> and in <m>y</m>. Be careful! Notice that you have really added <m>4(4) = 16</m> to 
the <m>x</m>-terms and <m>-6(1) = -6</m> to the <m>y</m>-terms on the left side of the equation.</p></li> 
    <li><p> Simplify each side.</p></li>    
    <li><p>Divide both sides by the constant, and simplify.</p></li>
  </ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Writing the Equation for a Hyperbola</title>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>Find the equation of the hyperbola shown.</p>
      <image source="act-9-4-2.png" width="60%"><description>Hyperbola with asymptotes</description></image>
  <p>The center of the hyperbola is <fillin/> </p>      
  <p>Sketch the central rectangle through the vertices.</p>
  <p>The length of the transverse axis is <m>2a= <fillin/></m>,  so <m>a=<fillin/></m>.</p>                      
  <p>The length of the conjugate axis is <m>2b= <fillin/></m>,  so <m>b=<fillin/></m>.</p>
  <p>The equation of the hyperbola is  <fillin characters="16"/> </p></li>

  <li><p>Write the equation of the hyperbola with center at <m>(6,-2)</m>, with <m>a=1</m> and <m>b=4</m>, opening up and down.</p>
      <p>Sketch the hyperbola on the grid.</p>
      <image source="act-2-2-2.png" width="60%"><description>10x10 Grid</description></image></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>In Activity 1, how do we sketch the asymptotes of the graph?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 2, what was the difference among the equations of the two hyperbolas and the ellipse?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 3, what were you cautioned to be careful about?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 4, how did you find <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Explain how to use the central rectangle for graphing a hyperbola.</p></li>
    <li><p>What do you need to know about a hyperbola to find the equations of its asymptotes?</p></li>
    <li><p>How can you find the center of a hyperbola whose equation is given in general form?</p></li>
    <li><p>Why does the graph of <m>\dfrac{x^2}{9} - \dfrac{y^2}{4} = 1</m> have no points with <m>x</m>-coordinates between -3 and 3?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>The graph of <m>Ax^2+Cy^2+Dx+Ey+F=0</m> is a hyperbola if</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>D</m> and <m>E</m> equal 0</li>
        <li><m>F=1</m></li>
        <li><m>AC=0</m></li>
        <li><m>AC \lt 0</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The hyperbola <m>~\dfrac{y^2}{4} - \dfrac{x^2}{9} = 1</m>  passes through the point</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>(2,0)</m></li>
        <li><m>(0,2)</m></li>
        <li><m>(0,3)</m></li>
        <li><m>(3,0)</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The center of the hyperbola <m>~\dfrac{(x-5)^2}{0} - \dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1~</m> is</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>(3,4)</m></li>
        <li><m>(-5,2)</m></li>
        <li><m>(5,-2)</m></li>
        <li><m>(3,-4)</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The slopes of the asymptotes of <m>~\dfrac{(x-5)^2}{0} - \dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1~</m> are</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\pm \dfrac{5}{2}</m></li>
        <li><m>\pm \dfrac{2}{5}</m></li>
        <li><m>\pm \dfrac{3}{4}</m></li>
        <li><m>\pm \dfrac{4}{3}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>
